            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Problem: More Accounts, More Chaos?</h2>
<p>When an advertising team expands from single to multi-account management, common issues emerge:</p>
<ul>
<li>Unclear budget allocation across accounts</li>
<li>Creative overlap leading to internal traffic issues</li>
<li>One account violation affecting others</li>
<li>No clear accountability when problems arise</li>
</ul>
<p>Account tiering is the starting point to solve these problems. Here's an actionable checklist you can implement immediately.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Define Your Tiering Dimensions</h2>
<p>There's no one-size-fits-all approach. Choose dimensions that match your business reality:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Best For</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>By Business Line</strong></td>
<td>Independent cost tracking for multiple products</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>By Channel</strong></td>
<td>Separate management for Meta, Google, TikTok</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>By Objective</strong></td>
<td>Isolating brand, conversion, and ROI strategies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>By Scale</strong></td>
<td>Focus on high-spend accounts, batch manage small ones</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong><br />
- [ ] Primary tiering dimension confirmed (1-2 dimensions recommended)<br />
- [ ] Business owners for each dimension identified</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Establish Account Classification Standards</h2>
<p>Further divide accounts by importance to allocate management resources:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Daily Spend</th>
<th>Inspection Frequency</th>
<th>Approval Level</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Core</strong></td>
<td>&gt;$3000</td>
<td>Daily real-time</td>
<td>Manager</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Priority</strong></td>
<td>$500-$3000</td>
<td>Every 2 days</td>
<td>Team Lead</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Standard</strong></td>
<td>&lt;$500</td>
<td>Weekly</td>
<td>Optimizer</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong><br />
- [ ] Account count per level completed<br />
- [ ] Each account labeled with its level<br />
- [ ] Inspection frequencies confirmed</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Build Account Profiles</h2>
<p>Every account needs a complete profile with:</p>
<ul>
<li>Account ID, name, advertiser name</li>
<li>Business line, channel, tier level</li>
<li>Account creation date, current balance, spend trends</li>
<li>Compliance history (any violations)</li>
<li>Associated Pages, Pixels, Business Managers</li>
<li>Owner and emergency contact</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong><br />
- [ ] Account profile spreadsheet established<br />
- [ ] Key data updated monthly<br />
- [ ] Profiles stored in shared team location</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Assign Permissions and Responsibilities</h2>
<p>Clear boundaries prevent confusion:</p>
<p><strong>Recommended Structure:</strong><br />
- <strong>Account Owner</strong>: Primary operator, responsible for performance<br />
- <strong>Finance Approver</strong>: Required for top-ups and budget changes<br />
- <strong>Risk Controller</strong>: Regular compliance checks (can be shared by team lead)</p>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong><br />
- [ ] Owner assigned to all accounts<br />
- [ ] Top-up procedures documented<br />
- [ ] Permission matrix diagram created</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Establish Alert and Review Systems</h2>
<p>Tiering enables differentiated monitoring—not one-size-fits-all:</p>
<p><strong>Core Accounts:</strong><br />
- Alert when balance drops below $500<br />
- Investigate immediately if spend exceeds forecast by 30%<br />
- Respond to policy violations within 24 hours</p>
<p><strong>Standard Accounts:</strong><br />
- Remind when balance drops below $200<br />
- Weekly spend summary<br />
- Monthly review only</p>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong><br />
- [ ] Alert rules configured in ad platforms<br />
- [ ] Alert channels confirmed (email/Feishu/Slack)<br />
- [ ] Weekly report template ready<br />
- [ ] Monthly review meetings scheduled</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Execution Timeline</h2>
<p>Implement your tiering system in this order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>This week</strong>: Inventory all existing accounts, fill in basic info</li>
<li><strong>Week 2</strong>: Confirm tiering dimensions and classification standards</li>
<li><strong>Week 3</strong>: Build account profiles, assign owners</li>
<li><strong>Week 4</strong>: Configure alert rules, start trial run</li>
<li><strong>Monthly</strong>: Review and iterate</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>Account tiering isn't about creating spreadsheets—it's about building team consensus on priority, resource allocation, and risk control for each account. Adjust granularity based on team size: 10 accounts need only two tiers, while 50 accounts require more detailed dimension design. Core principle: <strong>Focus on what matters, but don't lose control of the rest.</strong></p>
